  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAn expansive cutoff low was climbing northeastward toward the \nNortheast today as another trough was moving eastward from the \nWestern US. Warming is expected to take place this afternoon as the \nmain area of vorticity surrounding the upper low departs the area \nmeaning the mix of snow (with times of drizzle because of DGZ \nsaturation) with rain should just become rain this afternoon. At the \nbase of this approaching mid level trough, a moderate shortwave was \nmoving east-northeastward toward the area, but subsidence behind the \ndeparting upper low should keep the area mostly dry overnight. There \nis some indication in the RRFS, the ECMWF, and to some extent, the \nHRRR that some weak areas of weak precip may attempt to occur \nSaturday morning mainly north of US-6, but it'll need to overcome \nsome low level dry air. Temps appear fairly marginal for snow so \nwill lean rain at this juncture. Otherwise, the bulk of the area \nleans dry Saturday. There could be some 20 mph wind gusts to finish \nout the day Saturday as the upper low departs the area and weak \nmixing ensues. Another vort max rounds the base of the trough \nSaturday through the Southern Plains and approaches the area Sunday \nmorning. The general theme for much of these events where cold air \nhas attempted to wrap in on the back side thus far this winter has \nbeen for the cold air to be late to arrive. As 850 mb temperatures \napproach -6C on the backside of the precip shield, the tendency for \nthe precip shield later Sunday will be to dry out. It'll be a race \nagainst time though that maybe there could be a finish with snow on \nits back end after sunset Sunday. As cold air continues to push\nin and the column dries out, an area of increased gradient \nkeeps winds excited overnight and this will allow for the \npotential for gusts between 25 and 35 mph. \n\nAs mentioned above, strong drying within subsidence occurs on the \nbackside of the departing trough as high pressure to our south make \nits presence felt and mid level heights rise. Low level temperatures \nalso warm back above freezing. It's interesting to see the GFS lag \nbehind the other models including its AI counterpart for the mid \nweek trough approaching from the southwest. There's still enough \ndifferences in these models to indicate that certainty is low on \nwhether we get rain to end the end of next week. Between the NBM and \na blend of the long range ensembles and operational guidance, there \nseems to be some agreement that the Thursday night into Friday \nmorning time period presents the best chance to see rain. The GFS \nstill lags this and sees the best chance waiting until Friday \nnight/Saturday. It'll also be a breezy time period with 25 to 35 mph \ngusts possible.",
